  <section id="best-for">
    <h2>Who the Grand Floridian is best for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Anyone who wants an easy trip to Magic Kingdom.</strong> You can ride the resort monorail or take the boat. Theme Park View rooms look toward the park.</li>
      <li><strong>Families of five who don't want a suite.</strong> Most standard rooms sleep five, and they are usually in the outer buildings, which can feel a little quieter than the main building.</li>
      <li><strong>People who want a lot of dining without leaving the hotel.</strong> There is a character buffet, several table-service restaurants, a cafe, a quick-service grill and two lounges, plus a spa on site.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Think twice if…</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The nightly rate is the deciding factor.</strong> This is a Deluxe resort at the high end. In 2026, a Resort View room ran about $832 to $1,370 a night at full price, tax included.</li>
      <li><strong>Your parks are mostly EPCOT or Hollywood Studios.</strong> EPCOT takes a monorail change at the TTC. Hollywood Studios is a bus.</li>
      <li><strong>You want a completely quiet arrival.</strong> Renovations in select areas, including the porte cochere, continue through early 2027. Disney says you may see or hear work during the day or need to take another path.</li>
    </ul>
    <span class="ryan">[Ryan: who you think this resort is really for, from your own stays. Two or three sentences.]</span>
  </section>

